<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hundred Days' Reform</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hundred Days Reform, Hundred Days</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hundred Days' Reform was a programme of modernising reforms launched by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> in 1898, covering education, administration and the economy. It was ended after about a hundred days by a conservative coup backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>. Its failure is a key episode in the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform.jpg" alt="Hundred Days' Reform" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W._A._P._Martin_%26_faculty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Hundred Days' Reform, also called the Wuxu Reform, ran from 11 June to 21 September 1898. It followed the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> of 1894 and 1895, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to recognise Korean independence, cede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the Liaodong Peninsula and pay a large indemnity. Elites saw the defeat as proof that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>, which concentrated on weapons and arsenals, had failed, and that institutional change was needed.</p><p>Thinkers including Tan Sitong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao, influenced by the Meiji reforms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> needed more than technical improvements. Parts of the Qing government were alarmed enough to let Kang and Liang put their proposals to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>, who agreed to an ambitious programme.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Over about a hundred days the emperor issued a rapid series of edicts on education, administration, the economy and the military. They called for a modern school system teaching mathematics and science, gradual replacement of the traditional civil service examinations, a bureau for railways and mines, a modern army and naval academy, and a move towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> on the Meiji model. The Imperial University of Peking, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>, was established.</p><p>Conservative opposition was intense among senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> princes, Grand Councilors and provincial governors, who saw the changes to the examinations and the abolition of sinecures as threats to the social and political order. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> supported the principles but feared that sudden change would be disruptive and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and other powers would exploit any weakness. Later accounts say that Tan Sitong asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> to kill Ronglu and march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to arrest Cixi, but Yuan told Ronglu instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With the support of conservatives and the forces of Yuan and Ronglu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a> staged a coup in September 1898 and took over the government. Guangxu was put under house arrest until his death in 1908. The reforms were reversed, and six leading reformers, the Six Gentlemen of Wuxu, including Tan Sitong, were ordered executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where they founded the Baohuang Hui and worked unsuccessfully for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>.</p><p>Historians differ. The mainstream view sees heroic but disorganised reformers blocked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a>'s conservative faction, while some late 20th-century historians hold that Kang and his allies were dreamers unaware of political realities. The failure of the later reforms helped revolutionary forces, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> succeeded in 1911.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hundred Days' Reform” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1898 the Empress Dowager Cixi suppressed the <b>Hundred Days’ Reform</b>, placing the reformist Guangxu Emperor under house arrest and executing six of his reformist allies; this ended the most ambitious internal attempt at systemic renewal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat discredited the policy of borrowing techniques alone and led reformers to demand changes in institutions, in the <b>Hundred Days’ Reform</b> of 1898 and, after the Boxer Rebellion, in the New Policies of the 1900s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>He escaped less than a year later for the <b>Hundred Days</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ludendorff called it the 'black day of the German army'; the <b>Hundred Days</b> Offensive had begun.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Michel Ney, whom Napoleon called 'the bravest of the brave', was shot in Paris for treason after joining his former emperor during the <b>Hundred Days</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He regained power without a shot, beginning the <b>Hundred Days</b> that ended at Waterloo.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-26">26 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
